port61-nomq: module-owned IPC namespace and POSIX mqueue backend for 6.1

Status: M0 scaffold. The tree is a seeded template, not a working port. It is
not part of build/gki-targets.json and is not shipped by any release.

Provenance
----------
Base:            src/port61 (Android 14 / Linux 6.1), copied verbatim.
Added backend:   src/port515/mqueue.c and src/port515/mq_sysctl.c (Android 13 /
                 Linux 5.15), copied verbatim; the Makefile picks both up
                 automatically through its wildcard checks. 5.15 is the closer
                 template for these two files because the 6.1 kernel still has
                 current_time(), kern_unmount(), mnt_userns and
                 register_sysctl_table, while src/port66's 6.6 text uses
                 mnt_idmap, alloc_inode_sb and mnt_make_shortterm, none of which
                 exist in 6.1 (checked against the target device's kallsyms).
Everything else: still the src/port61 text, which claims the native 6.1 IPC
                 namespace and POSIX mqueue objects.

Why this target exists
----------------------
src/port61 assumes the 6.1 GKI baseline, where CONFIG_POSIX_MQUEUE=y and
CONFIG_IPC_NS=y. A vendor kernel built with CONFIG_SYSVIPC=n and
CONFIG_POSIX_MQUEUE=n has no such objects, so the module imports
init_ipc_ns, mq_lock, mq_init_ns, mq_clear_sbinfo, mq_put_mnt,
setup_mq_sysctls and retire_mq_sysctls, none of which exist there; the kernel
rejects the load with -ENOENT. See
build/device-profiles/6.1.138-android14-11.json for the recorded evidence.

Every other port in this repository already owns these objects:
src/port515, src/port510, src/port66, src/port and src/port618 all define
DEFINE_SPINLOCK(mq_lock), struct ipc_namespace init_ipc_ns, their own
copy_ipcs/free_ipc_ns/ipcns_operations, and ship mqueue.c plus mq_sysctl.c.
src/port61 is the only port that instead reuses the kernel's native objects.

Work still to do (see build/port61-nomq.json for the full change list)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
1. names.h: add "#define init_ipc_ns ds_init_ipc_ns" and
   "#define mq_lock ds_mq_lock", as src/port66/names.h does. Consider the same
   for mq_init_ns / mq_clear_sbinfo / mq_put_mnt, whose plain names still exist
   in a kernel that ships native POSIX mqueue (the DDK kernel does).
2. ipc.c: define init_ipc_ns and mq_lock, own copy_ipcs/free_ipc_ns/
   ipcns_operations, stop importing the native ones, and drop the
   ds_ipc_ensure/ds_ipc_empty_set_init machinery that only existed to attach to
   native objects.
3. ipc-task.c, ipc_sysctl.c, ipc-util.c/h, ipc-backend.h, exit.c, main.c,
   fork.c, pid.c, sem.c, shm.c: take the src/port515 hunks that concern the own
   backend only. Do not back-port 6.6-only changes, in particular
   nsproxy.c's exec_task_namespaces(), the CLONE_VM time namespace handling and
   src/port66/ipc.c's mnt_make_shortterm(); src/port61 keeps the 6.1 forms.
4. mqueue.c and mq_sysctl.c: keep the 5.15 VFS/sysctl call forms and only fix
   what 6.1 actually changed; upstream 6.1 ipc/mqueue.c is the reference.
5. hooks.c: add the six mq syscall slots, drop the four hooks that wrap the
   native IPC namespace entries, and keep src/port61's KCFI-style entry checks
   and raw branch encodings (the device kernel is KCFI, not classic CFI).

Acceptance gates
----------------
- the target builds clean in DDK android14-6.1-20260313,
- the built module imports none of the blocking symbols listed in the device
  profile and does define ds_init_ipc_ns / ds_mq_lock / ds_copy_ipcs /
  ds_ipcns_operations (scripts/port61-nomq-gate.py enforces both),
- BTF layouts for nsproxy, ipc_namespace, pid_namespace and pid match the
  device profile,
- the VM regression runs on a 6.1 kernel built without SYSVIPC,
  POSIX_MQUEUE, PID_NS, USER_NS and DEVTMPFS,
- the activate path is confirmed on the device, where the KCFI entry checks
  actually run.
